raw:
Reasoning first.

The request has one operational goal: reproduce the *daily streamflow hydrograph* at a USGS gauge and report NSE. That is a routed, basin-integrated discharge target. The framework runs independent single-column 1-D ELM points with NO lateral transport, NO routing, and NO native integrated streamflow. ELM can generate the *runoff-production* terms per column (QRUNOFF = surface runoff QOVER + subsurface drainage QDRAI), but converting a set of column runoff fluxes into a discharge time series at a gauge requires (a) area-weighted aggregation over the contributing area and (b) a routing/timing step to translate grid runoff into channel flow. Neither exists in the stack. Daily NSE is especially sensitive to *timing* (routing delay, snowmelt pulse propagation), which the framework cannot represent at all. So the PRIMARY mechanism the question asks for is not representable — verdict is infeasible as posed.

What the stack CAN do here: the American River / upper Naches is a snowmelt-driven, high-relief basin (352–1977 m). NLDAS-2 at 12 km does resolve orographic precip/temperature across that relief, and snow/SWE is in the default output. So we can legitimately produce a physically-reasonable *basin runoff-generation estimate* by stratifying columns across the elevation/precip gradient and the rain-snow transition, spin them up (recharge/baseflow require warm start), and sum QRUNOFF as an unrouted water-balance estimate. We can compare monthly/annual runoff volume and the seasonal (snowmelt-timing) *shape* qualitatively against the gauge — but NOT daily NSE. I will design that fallback and be explicit that daily NSE requires a routing/aggregation capability.

Note: candidate_drivers lists only "forcing," so the exploratory stratification axis is the NLDAS elevation/precip gradient plus native SSURGO soil; elevation here acts through forcing (precip/temperature/snow), which NLDAS DOES resolve at 12 km, so it is a legitimate axis (subject to the sub-12 km caveat).

